1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,852
A required check on main
has been red for five days.

2
00:00:07,892 --> 00:00:13,006
A required check is a test result that
must pass before a change may be merged.

3
00:00:13,046 --> 00:00:18,486
One step fails on the runner, the machine
that runs the job, with exit code 128, the

4
00:00:18,526 --> 00:00:23,704
program's way of reporting a failure. The
script behind that step works on every

5
00:00:23,744 --> 00:00:29,119
laptop in the team. Re-runs fail identically.
A developer with a harmless change is

6
00:00:29,159 --> 00:00:33,033
blocked, has a plausible culprit,
and asks for the shortcut.

7
00:00:33,373 --> 00:00:36,987
"Flaky" is a diagnosis, and it
has a property you can test.

8
00:00:37,027 --> 00:00:42,127
A flaky failure comes and goes: it doesn't
repeat identically, and it doesn't start

9
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:47,266
on one day and then fail on every run. This
failure repeats identically, and it has

10
00:00:47,306 --> 00:00:52,468
failed on every run for five days. Today
you refuse the word until the investigation

11
00:00:52,508 --> 00:00:57,484
order has been followed, and you rebuild
the runner's view of the repository with

12
00:00:57,524 --> 00:01:02,129
Git alone. And hold on to one question:
the laptops pass, the runner fails.

13
00:01:02,169 --> 00:01:03,243
Can both be right?

14
00:01:05,083 --> 00:01:09,036
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:09,076 --> 00:01:13,225
This is a debrief of Incident 7,
incidents/07-ci-passes-locally.

16
00:01:13,265 --> 00:01:18,265
You need to have generated it, read its
evidence directory, and attempted it.

17
00:01:18,305 --> 00:01:23,698
If you haven't, please stop the video here.
The cause is named in the next section.

18
00:01:26,038 --> 00:01:31,003
A word about the evidence. Incident 7 comes
with a workflow file and a description

19
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:36,068
of a failed run. A workflow file tells
GitHub Actions, GitHub's automation service,

20
00:01:36,108 --> 00:01:40,341
which jobs to run on an event. Both
were constructed for the exercise.

21
00:01:40,381 --> 00:01:45,284
Nothing was captured from GitHub, by the
authors of the course or for this video,

22
00:01:45,324 --> 00:01:47,665
and no GitHub output appears on screen.

23
00:01:48,005 --> 00:01:52,642
The evidence that can be reproduced is on
the Git side, and that's the point of the

24
00:01:52,682 --> 00:01:56,924
drill. For a large class of "passes
locally, fails on Actions" problems, the

25
00:01:56,964 --> 00:01:59,403
difference is the
repository the runner had.

26
00:01:59,743 --> 00:02:03,883
From video 153 you have the
documented causes of that class.

27
00:02:03,923 --> 00:02:09,665
From video 154, the debugging instruments.
This incident is also the third scenario

28
00:02:09,705 --> 00:02:13,566
of the senior standard: "GitHub
Actions suddenly fails".

29
00:02:15,406 --> 00:02:16,984
Here's what you'll walk away with.

30
00:02:17,324 --> 00:02:18,736
After this video you can:

31
00:02:19,076 --> 00:02:23,611
Refuse "flaky runner" as a diagnosis
until the investigation order has been

32
00:02:23,651 --> 00:02:24,160
followed.

33
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:28,833
Establish which commit the run tested and
what the runner's repository contained.

34
00:02:29,173 --> 00:02:31,833
Reproduce the runner's view
locally with Git alone.

35
00:02:32,173 --> 00:02:35,048
Fix the cause and predict
what the next run will test.

36
00:02:35,388 --> 00:02:40,096
Handle "nothing changed and CI fails" by
listing what can change without a commit.

37
00:02:41,936 --> 00:02:47,269
"Suddenly" is a claim to test first. Either
something in the repository changed: a

38
00:02:47,309 --> 00:02:52,774
commit to the workflow, the lock file or
the code. A commit is one saved snapshot of

39
00:02:52,814 --> 00:02:58,212
the project. Or something outside it did:
a runner image, an action behind a moving

40
00:02:58,252 --> 00:03:03,323
tag, a secret, a cache. git log --oneline
-- .github/workflows and the list of

41
00:03:03,363 --> 00:03:06,010
recent runs separate the two in a minute.

42
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:10,621
The first answers "where does green turn
red". The second answers "which commit did

43
00:03:10,661 --> 00:03:12,179
the run test, on which event".

44
00:03:12,519 --> 00:03:17,278
Then a fixed order. The investigation goes
from "did the right thing start" to "what

45
00:03:17,318 --> 00:03:21,449
did it say". An answer early in the
list makes the later ones irrelevant.

46
00:03:24,789 --> 00:03:30,382
Applied to Incident 7. Areas 1 to 8 are
answered from the workflow file and the run

47
00:03:30,422 --> 00:03:31,807
report without a log.

48
00:03:32,147 --> 00:03:37,506
One event, a read-only token, a fixed runner
label, no environment, no secrets, both

49
00:03:37,546 --> 00:03:42,905
actions pinned by commit SHA. Area 9 gives
one line, and it's a Git message: "fatal:

50
00:03:42,945 --> 00:03:48,240
No names found, cannot describe anything."
That points at the repository the runner

51
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:50,554
had, and Git can build the same one.

52
00:03:50,894 --> 00:03:54,943
Before I name the cause, name
it yourself. Say it out loud.

53
00:03:59,783 --> 00:04:05,162
The root cause. actions/checkout fetches
one commit and no tags by default, and git

54
00:04:05,202 --> 00:04:10,386
describe needs history and tags.
actions/checkout is the action that fetches the

55
00:04:10,426 --> 00:04:15,675
repository onto the runner. A tag is a
fixed name for one commit, here a release,

56
00:04:15,715 --> 00:04:19,593
and git describe derives a
version from the newest such tag.

57
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:25,304
The README of the checkout action, version
7.0.1, says it fetches a single commit by

58
00:04:25,344 --> 00:04:30,522
default, with fetch-tags off, and that
fetch-depth: 0 fetches all history for all

59
00:04:30,562 --> 00:04:35,805
branches and tags. The step was added
without changing the checkout it depends on.

60
00:04:35,845 --> 00:04:38,639
Layer: GitHub Actions,
a documented default.

61
00:04:38,979 --> 00:04:43,985
Why both can be right. "It passes on my
machine" and "it fails on the runner" are

62
00:04:44,025 --> 00:04:48,470
both true, at the same commit, because
the commit isn't the whole input.

63
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:53,205
A full clone, a complete copy of the
repository, has five commits and a tag.

64
00:04:53,545 --> 00:04:58,259
The runner's clone has one commit and no
tag. Same commit ID, different repository

65
00:04:58,299 --> 00:05:01,621
around it. That's the question
from the opening, answered.

66
00:05:01,961 --> 00:05:03,642
Two habits complete the standard.

67
00:05:03,982 --> 00:05:08,013
Look behind the first failure.
The job never reached its tests.

68
00:05:08,053 --> 00:05:13,376
A second defect waits behind the first: a
template tracked under one spelling while

69
00:05:13,416 --> 00:05:16,994
the code opens it under another,
differing only in case.

70
00:05:17,034 --> 00:05:22,357
That works only on a filesystem that ignores
case. A Mac's default filesystem does.

71
00:05:22,397 --> 00:05:23,714
The runner's doesn't.

72
00:05:24,054 --> 00:05:28,991
And refuse the shortcut. A red required
check isn't overridden by an administrator

73
00:05:29,031 --> 00:05:33,846
because the change "is only a docstring".
The answer to "merge it anyway" is no,

74
00:05:33,886 --> 00:05:34,817
with the reason.

75
00:05:35,157 --> 00:05:39,874
What "fixed" means here. The honest status
after the fix is pushed: "cause removed

76
00:05:39,914 --> 00:05:43,644
in the files; confirmed when the
run on the fix branch is green."

77
00:05:43,684 --> 00:05:47,937
The files alone prove that the cause
was removed, not that the job passes.

78
00:05:47,977 --> 00:05:51,765
Verification is a green run on the
fix branch, observed on GitHub.

79
00:05:52,105 --> 00:05:58,113
Quick quiz. The fix is pushed. To see it
tested, do you A, re-run the failed run, or

80
00:05:58,153 --> 00:06:01,929
B, wait for a new run on the
fix branch? Your answer?

81
00:06:06,769 --> 00:06:12,572
Re-running. The answer is B. A re-run
reuses the original commit and ref, so it

82
00:06:12,612 --> 00:06:18,637
can't test a fix. Re-run with gh run rerun
<run-id> --failed --debug only when the

83
00:06:18,677 --> 00:06:19,969
log is not enough.

84
00:06:20,309 --> 00:06:25,829
Severity. SEV 3: a team is blocked. The
line for the postmortem, the written record

85
00:06:25,869 --> 00:06:31,255
of the incident, is the uncomfortable one:
five days of red on main hid a second,

86
00:06:31,295 --> 00:06:32,059
real defect.

87
00:06:33,899 --> 00:06:38,543
A picture helps. Think of the runner as
a colleague who joined this morning, was

88
00:06:38,583 --> 00:06:43,403
handed a single printed page of the project,
the latest one, and was asked to state

89
00:06:43,443 --> 00:06:48,204
the version number of the book. They can't.
The version is derived from a bookmark

90
00:06:48,244 --> 00:06:52,771
that sits many pages back, and they
weren't given those pages or the bookmark.

91
00:06:53,111 --> 00:06:57,036
The colleague isn't flaky. They'll
give the same answer every time.

92
00:06:57,076 --> 00:07:01,890
The mistake was in what they were handed,
and it was a deliberate default: handing

93
00:07:01,930 --> 00:07:03,192
over one page is fast.

94
00:07:03,532 --> 00:07:08,383
Where the picture breaks: a new colleague
would ask for the rest of the book.

95
00:07:08,423 --> 00:07:12,703
A runner does what the workflow file
says and reports exit code 128.

96
00:07:13,043 --> 00:07:17,717
For "suddenly fails", sort every candidate
cause into two columns: changes you can

97
00:07:17,757 --> 00:07:22,546
see with git log, and changes you can't.
The first column is short and is checked in

98
00:07:22,586 --> 00:07:27,260
a minute. If it's empty for the day the
failure began, the cause is in the second.

99
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,931
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Draw
those two columns and sort four causes: a

100
00:07:32,971 --> 00:07:37,918
commit to the lock file, a moved runner
image, a rotated secret, a commit to a

101
00:07:37,958 --> 00:07:41,179
script that a step runs.
Say your answers out loud.

102
00:07:47,519 --> 00:07:52,325
Check your paper. The two commits are on
the left. The runner image and the secret

103
00:07:52,365 --> 00:07:57,170
are on the right. In Incident 7 the first
red run coincides with a commit: the one

104
00:07:57,210 --> 00:08:01,898
that added the version step. So the cause
is reachable from the left column, and

105
00:08:01,938 --> 00:08:06,862
"flaky" is excluded before the log is read.
The last line of the right column is the

106
00:08:06,902 --> 00:08:11,766
bridge between the two: the commit is the
same everywhere, and what surrounds it on

107
00:08:11,806 --> 00:08:12,830
the runner is not.

108
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:18,187
Into the lab. From here on the
screen shows the solution.

109
00:08:20,527 --> 00:08:21,707
It does pass locally.

110
00:08:22,047 --> 00:08:28,973
The script runs git describe --tags --match
"v*" and prints a version built from the

111
00:08:29,013 --> 00:08:35,607
tag v1.4.0, three commits back. Exit status
0. The reporter is right about their

112
00:08:35,647 --> 00:08:36,271
machine.

113
00:08:36,611 --> 00:08:38,357
The workflow, and when it changed.

114
00:08:38,697 --> 00:08:42,373
The checkout step has no options:
the line after it is empty.

115
00:08:42,413 --> 00:08:47,431
And the history of the two files: the
workflow was added in one commit, the version

116
00:08:47,471 --> 00:08:52,183
step in a later one, 261eb76. That's the
left column of the diagram, answered.

117
00:08:52,223 --> 00:08:55,230
The first red run and this
commit belong together.

118
00:08:55,570 --> 00:09:00,725
The runner's clone, made with Git. Predict
three things: how many commits, how many

119
00:09:00,765 --> 00:09:03,854
tags, and what the script
prints. Say it out loud.

120
00:09:08,694 --> 00:09:13,929
A shallow repository: a clone that holds
only the newest commits and treats the

121
00:09:13,969 --> 00:09:19,538
history as ending there. One commit. No
tags. And the script prints the same line as

122
00:09:19,578 --> 00:09:25,013
the run report, with exit status 128. The
failure is reproduced on a Mac, with Git

123
00:09:25,053 --> 00:09:28,418
alone, with no runner
involved. It's deterministic.

124
00:09:28,758 --> 00:09:32,080
Proof of the remedy. What
does fetch-depth: 0 change?

125
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:35,505
The same clone, with all
history and the tags fetched.

126
00:09:35,845 --> 00:09:39,139
Five commits, one tag, and the
script prints the version.

127
00:09:39,179 --> 00:09:42,181
The remedy is proven before
the workflow is touched.

128
00:09:42,521 --> 00:09:47,572
Look behind the first failure. Would the
tests pass on Linux once the version step

129
00:09:47,612 --> 00:09:50,242
is fixed? Look at names
as Git stores them.

130
00:09:50,582 --> 00:09:56,184
Git tracks summary.md.tmpl with a lower-case
s. The code opens a name with a capital

131
00:09:56,224 --> 00:10:01,692
S. The pickaxe, a search for commits that
changed how often a string occurs, names

132
00:10:01,732 --> 00:10:07,200
the commit that introduced the spelling:
367b8fb, made while main was already red.

133
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:12,775
On a filesystem that ignores case this
works. On the runner it would have failed as

134
00:10:12,815 --> 00:10:14,723
soon as the job got that far.

135
00:10:15,063 --> 00:10:19,528
The fix: one branch, one pull request.
🟡 CAUTION: a commit and a push of a new

136
00:10:19,568 --> 00:10:23,975
branch. A pull request is GitHub's
proposal to merge one branch into another.

137
00:10:24,315 --> 00:10:28,374
Two changes in the diff. The
checkout step gains fetch-depth: 0.

138
00:10:28,414 --> 00:10:33,753
The template name is spelled as it's tracked.
Note what didn't change: the action is

139
00:10:33,793 --> 00:10:35,930
still pinned to a full commit SHA.

140
00:10:39,270 --> 00:10:43,857
The check passes. Read what it checks:
the files. It can't observe a run.

141
00:10:44,197 --> 00:10:48,133
So predict what the next run will
test, which is objective four.

142
00:10:48,173 --> 00:10:53,165
A pull_request run for this branch checks
out the merge ref, the head merged into

143
00:10:53,205 --> 00:10:57,949
the current base, with full history and
tags. The version step finds the tag.

144
00:10:57,989 --> 00:11:01,117
The tests open the template
under its tracked name.

145
00:11:01,157 --> 00:11:02,360
That's a prediction.

146
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:05,735
The status to report is:
cause removed in the files.

147
00:11:05,775 --> 00:11:08,691
Confirmed when the run on
the fix branch is green.

148
00:11:09,031 --> 00:11:12,063
The answer to the reporter.
No administrative merge.

149
00:11:12,103 --> 00:11:16,434
The failure isn't flaky: it repeats,
it began with a commit, and it can be

150
00:11:16,474 --> 00:11:20,510
reproduced on a laptop in six commands.
The fix is in a pull request.

151
00:11:20,550 --> 00:11:23,641
And the docstring change
isn't held up by the runner.

152
00:11:23,681 --> 00:11:28,603
It's held up by a red default branch that
should have been treated as an incident on

153
00:11:28,643 --> 00:11:29,430
its first day.

154
00:11:31,270 --> 00:11:32,918
Five mistakes to watch for.

155
00:11:33,258 --> 00:11:38,149
Diagnosing "flaky" from "it passes locally".
Root cause: the local clone has full

156
00:11:38,189 --> 00:11:43,141
history and tags and the runner's checkout
has one commit and no tags, so the same

157
00:11:43,181 --> 00:11:45,454
commit runs in different repositories.

158
00:11:45,794 --> 00:11:50,866
Re-running to test a theory. Root cause:
a re-run reuses the original commit and

159
00:11:50,906 --> 00:11:53,103
ref, so it repeats the same inputs.

160
00:11:53,443 --> 00:11:58,729
Stopping at the first failure. Root cause:
the job never reached its tests, so every

161
00:11:58,769 --> 00:12:01,773
defect behind the failing
step stayed invisible.

162
00:12:02,113 --> 00:12:05,344
Overriding a red required
check for a "harmless" change.

163
00:12:05,384 --> 00:12:09,959
Root cause: the check is the control for
the branch, and bypassing it makes red

164
00:12:09,999 --> 00:12:12,120
normal and hides real failures in it.

165
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:17,173
Reporting "fixed" from the files. Root
cause: the files show that the cause was

166
00:12:17,213 --> 00:12:21,385
removed; only a green run on the fix
branch shows that the job passes.

167
00:12:23,225 --> 00:12:28,238
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation-reporting
service derives its version from the

168
00:12:28,278 --> 00:12:33,166
newest release tag and stamps it on every
report. A week after that feature was

169
00:12:33,206 --> 00:12:38,406
added, a developer asks an administrator to
bypass the red check for a documentation

170
00:12:38,446 --> 00:12:41,712
change, "because the runner
has been flaky all week".

171
00:12:42,052 --> 00:12:47,375
The administrator opens the list of runs
first. Green turns red at one commit, and

172
00:12:47,415 --> 00:12:52,477
stays red. He reads the workflow file
from the top: event, permissions, runner

173
00:12:52,517 --> 00:12:57,578
label, action versions. Nothing moves
there. Then the one line the failed step

174
00:12:57,618 --> 00:13:03,072
printed, a message from Git. A depth-one
clone without tags on his own machine gives

175
00:13:03,112 --> 00:13:03,988
the same line.

176
00:13:04,328 --> 00:13:09,282
He doesn't bypass. He opens a pull request
with the checkout option, and because the

177
00:13:09,322 --> 00:13:13,979
job hasn't reached its tests for a week,
he also asks what else changed in that

178
00:13:14,019 --> 00:13:18,854
week. One commit moved a template path into
a constant, with a capital letter that

179
00:13:18,894 --> 00:13:20,638
the tracked file doesn't have.

180
00:13:20,978 --> 00:13:25,823
Both fixes go in together. In the retrospective
the team writes down the two rules

181
00:13:25,863 --> 00:13:30,649
from the textbook. A workflow change that
adds a tool is reviewed with the inputs

182
00:13:30,689 --> 00:13:34,998
the tool reads. And a red default
branch is an incident on its first day.

183
00:13:36,838 --> 00:13:43,964
Your turn. Do Lab 37.4, "CI works locally
but fails on GitHub Actions (incident 7)",

184
00:13:44,004 --> 00:13:50,789
in lab-manual/m37-incident-drills-platform.md,
from a freshly generated sandbox.

185
00:13:51,129 --> 00:13:55,864
Before you open the evidence, write the
twelve areas of the investigation order down

186
00:13:55,904 --> 00:14:00,582
the side of a page. Answer each from the
workflow file and the run description, and

187
00:14:00,622 --> 00:14:05,016
mark where the first informative answer
appears. Before you build the runner's

188
00:14:05,056 --> 00:14:08,767
clone, predict its commit count,
its tags and the script's output.

189
00:14:08,807 --> 00:14:13,315
After the fix, write the status sentence
you would send, with what is proven and

190
00:14:13,355 --> 00:14:14,224
what is not yet.

191
00:14:14,564 --> 00:14:21,424
The challenge is Lab 38.5, "Senior standard
3: GitHub Actions suddenly fails", in

192
00:14:21,464 --> 00:14:25,171
lab-manual/m38-communication-postmortems.md.

193
00:14:27,011 --> 00:14:29,065
Read the question, then answer out loud.

194
00:14:33,905 --> 00:14:38,682
A strong answer starts with the second
half, because it frames the first: say what

195
00:14:38,722 --> 00:14:43,617
else, besides the commit, is an input to a
run, with at least two examples about Git

196
00:14:43,657 --> 00:14:48,258
and one that isn't. Then say what you
check first and why: which commit the run

197
00:14:48,298 --> 00:14:52,077
tested, on which event, and what
the runner's checkout contained.

198
00:14:52,117 --> 00:14:55,719
Name the read-only way to reproduce
the runner's view locally.

199
00:14:55,759 --> 00:15:00,654
Finish with what you wouldn't accept as a
diagnosis without evidence, and the status

200
00:15:00,694 --> 00:15:02,593
you'd report after pushing a fix.

201
00:15:04,433 --> 00:15:07,622
Let's land this. Say each
line in your own words.

202
00:15:07,962 --> 00:15:12,569
"Suddenly fails" is tested first: either
a commit changed something, or something

203
00:15:12,609 --> 00:15:17,216
outside the repository did; the list of
runs and git log on the workflow separate

204
00:15:17,256 --> 00:15:17,675
the two.

205
00:15:18,015 --> 00:15:22,866
The investigation follows a fixed order of
twelve areas; an early answer makes later

206
00:15:22,906 --> 00:15:23,798
ones irrelevant.

207
00:15:24,138 --> 00:15:28,771
The default checkout is one commit without
tags, so anything that needs history or

208
00:15:28,811 --> 00:15:30,993
tags behaves differently on the runner.

209
00:15:31,333 --> 00:15:35,782
The runner's view can be rebuilt with a
